 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "TAKB.1 Managing the speaking process: begins and ends a conversation with suitable greeting expressions; makes eye contact. Ali looks his teacher in the eyes and says “Good afternoon!”, and closes the day with “Good night!”",
   "SAB.1 Using the concept of time appropriately in daily life (SAB.1.a Describes what is done in the morning, at midday, in the evening and at night): walking the dog in the morning, the market at midday, coming home in the evening, sleep at night; chooses the greeting that fits the time using sky clues (the low sun in the morning, the sun overhead at midday, the lit street lamps in the evening, the moon at night). Matches the sample activity in the programme: day and night pictures are examined and time-appropriate greetings are practised.",
   "skill: SBAB1 Perceiving Time and Chronological Thinking: puts the parts of the day in before-and-after order (the ordering game at the end of the story: morning, midday, night).",
   "E2.4 Confidence: after an embarrassing moment, grows braver step by step; in the evening he is the one who greets first.",
   "SDB2.1 Communication and D14 Respect: shows respect and warmth by greeting neighbours, teacher and family with suitable words."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Read the title: “Good Morning, Good Night.” Ask: “Do we say these two things at the same time? When do we say each one?”",
   "Look at the sky through the window or in a picture: “Is it morning, midday, evening or night now? How can you tell?”",
   "Ask: “What do we say when we wake up in the morning? What do we say when we go to bed?” Listen to the answers without correcting them."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "At the end of page 1, pause when Ali says “Good night!” Wait for the children's reaction and let them laugh: “Is that what we say in the morning?”",
   "On page 2, name Ali's feeling: “Ali felt embarrassed, his cheeks went warm. What happens in your body when you feel embarrassed?” Touch Granny's and Grandpa's kind words.",
   "On page 3, say that both options in the prediction question are good ideas. Repeat Grandpa's secret together: “Look at the sky, find the greeting!”",
   "Compare the sun on pages 1 and 3: in the morning the sun is low, at midday it is right overhead.",
   "On page 4, count Ali's steps together. Before the greeting, do the “look in their eyes, smile” action with the child, facing each other.",
   "On page 6, let the child match each sky clue with the right person; at each match, say the greeting out loud together.",
   "On the last page, count the stars one by one with a finger and whisper “good night”.",
   "On the last page, say that “Good night” is said when going to bed or when leaving, and that we can also say “Hello” at any time of day.",
   "Do not force shy children to say hello. Waving, smiling and making eye contact are greetings too; courage comes step by step."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "What did Ali say to Granny Nazlı in the morning? Why did he feel embarrassed afterwards?",
   "What was Grandpa's secret? What do we say when the sun is low, when it is right overhead, when the street lamps are on and when the moon is out?",
   "Why was Ali the first to say hello in the evening? What helped him?"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Look at the Sky, Find the Greeting",
   "sure": "15 min",
   "materyal": "Four big sky cards (rising sun, sun overhead, setting sun with a lit street lamp, moon and stars); activity pictures showing four moments of the day (waking up, lunch, coming home, going to bed); string and pegs",
   "adim": [
    "Put the sky cards in the middle of the circle and teach the rhyme: “The sun is up: Good morning! The sun's overhead: Good afternoon! The sun's gone down: Good evening! The moon is out: Good night!”",
    "Hold up one card. The children look their neighbour in the eyes, smile and say the greeting for that time; their friend replies with the same greeting.",
    "Mix up the activity pictures. The children hang them on the string in before-and-after order and say the right greeting for each picture.",
    "In the reflection circle, ask: “Who did you greet today, and how? What can you do when you feel embarrassed?” Offer shy children the option of waving."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Who did you say good morning to first at home this morning?",
   "How do we know which greeting to use when we look at the sky?",
   "What did Ali do when he felt embarrassed? What do you do when you feel embarrassed?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Greetings from the window",
   "d": "Over one day, look at the sky from the window with your child four times (morning, midday, evening, night). Talk about where the sun or the moon is, then give everyone at home the greeting for that time. At night, before bed, say “Good night” together to the moon, the stars and everyone at home from the window."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “Good Morning, Good Night.” Sleepy Ali says “Good night!” to his neighbour one morning and feels embarrassed; with his grandpa's secret, “Look at the sky, find the greeting!”, he finds the right greeting all day long and grows braver. The story supports time-appropriate greetings, making eye contact and the concepts of morning, midday, evening and night. At home, instead of pushing your child to say hello, set an example yourself; waving and smiling are a good start too. “Hello” can be said at any time of day; “Good night” is mostly said when saying goodbye at night or going to bed."
 },
